"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was 4.7. The surgical findings included a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2), pT3a stage with extraprostatic extension, positive surgical margins, and the absence of seminal vesicle invasion and lymphovascular invasion. The absence of lymph node removal and missing nodal sampling widen the uncertainty regarding nodal status. The biopsy showed a Gleason 3+3 (ISUP grade group 1), differing from the prostatectomy specimen.\n\nCountervailing factors include the absence of lymphovascular invasion and seminal vesicle invasion. The postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory are unknown, which significantly limits prognostic certainty. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and trajectory would be crucial to revise this assessment. The current heuristic horizon suggests a range for recurrence. The fixed estimated horizon is 63.37 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 41.19–85.55 months; its coverage has not been validated."
